<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Dutch Gold Coast</h1><p class="st-meta">1598 CE – 1872 CE · capital Elmina</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Dutch forts on the coast of present-day Ghana, run from Elmina for nearly three centuries as a base for the gold and slave trades.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> merchants began trading on the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast</a> in the 1590s, challenging the Portuguese monopoly. In 1612, at the invitation of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-asebu">Kingdom of Asebu</a>, they built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-nassau-gold-coast">Fort Nassau</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moree">Moree</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elmina">Elmina</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-gold-coast">Portuguese Gold Coast</a> in 1637 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axim">Axim</a> in 1642, making Elmina its headquarters.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A director-general at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elmina">Elmina</a> governed a chain of forts and lodges along the coast, trading cloth, guns and metal goods for gold and, from the late 17th century, enslaved Africans shipped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colonies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Relations with inland powers such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/denkyira">Denkyira</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a> were vital; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a> held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elmina-note">Elmina Note</a>, a rent paid by the Dutch. In the 1830s and 1840s the Dutch recruited soldiers there for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a> army.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1868 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and British exchanged forts to consolidate their zones, and in 1872 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> handed its remaining posts to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>. They became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast Colony</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elmina">Elmina Castle</a> is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dutch-gold-coast.jpg" alt="Dutch Gold Coast" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Flag_of_the_Dutch_West_India_Company.png: *Flag_of_the_Netherlands.svg: Zscout370 derivative work: Fentener van Vliss... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Dutch_West_India_Company.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1598 CE – 1872 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Elmina</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Dutch Reformed Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dutch</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ghana</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dutch Guinea, Nederlandse Bezittingen ter Kuste van Guinea</dd></dl></aside></div>
